      "text_before_first_found_rule": "suit commenced without leave given during a federally-legislated moratorium is a nullity. So is a statutory suit (even a proposed class action begun) without the leave to sue which legislation imposes.\n\nOnce litigation has got as far as the Supreme Court of Canada and is at an end, it is proper for the trial court to order that no new documents be filed by the plaintiff. That is ordered from time to time with persistent or vexatious litigants. An existing consent suit by one person for a narrow purpose should not be used by the opposing party for very different new complaints, albeit between the same parties about the same\n\ncompany, but that does not remove the court's jurisdiction to give the new claimant relief. A consent order is enough consent to an Applications Judge hearing a matter like an appeal. An Applications Judge cannot givecertiorari, but can give a declaration. When a suit, even for divorce, is commenced in one judicial centre, any motion in it should\n\nbe brought in the same judicial centre, especially if there are lawyers in that judicial centre experienced in that type of litigation. *How to Start an Action*",
      "text_before_first_found_rule_note": "probable commentary of a rule whose own text is on pages not in the file; attribution not verified",
